"Executive summary\n- Symptom: 100% ICMP packet loss in both directions between remote-worker (10.1.0.2) and server (10.0.1.2), i.e., complete L3 reachability failure end to end.\n- Healthy on source side: Remote-worker interface and routing are correct; ARP to the first hop is present; ping to 10.1.0.1 (branch-router) succeeds.\n- Likely fault domain: At or beyond the branch firewall hop between 10.0.0.0/30 (outside) and 10.0.1.0/24 (inside). Traceroute reaches hop 1 (10.1.0.1) but receives no replies from hop 2 onward, consistent with firewall drop/policy or forwarding/routing issue on the firewall/adjacent links.\n- Visibility gaps: Multiple collection failures (timeouts/errors) for iptables/nft and interface stats; firewall policy and server NIC state are unknown from the provided data.\n\nKey evidence\n- Remote-worker L2/L3 looks good:\n  - Interface: eth0 UP/LOWER_UP at 10.1.0.2/30 (toward branch-router).\n  - Routing: 10.0.1.0/24 and other 10.0.x/24 via next-hop 10.1.0.1 on eth0. ip route get 10.0.1.2 resolves via 10.1.0.1 from 10.1.0.2.\n  - ARP: Neighbor entry exists for 10.1.0.1 (STALE with probes) \u2014 no ARP failure reported.\n  - Ping: 10.1.0.1 reachable with 0% loss.\n- End-to-end tests:\n  - Ping to 10.0.1.2: 100% loss.\n  - Traceroute: either complete timeout or stops after hop 1 (10.1.0.1). No ICMP time-exceeded/unreachable from hop 2 (expected branch-fw outside 10.0.0.2), indicating drop/silence at or past the firewall.\n- Data gaps:\n  - iptables/nft rules retrieval failed on multiple devices (\u201ccommand not found\u201d or exit status 1).\n  - ip -s link show dev eth0 failed (BusyBox usage displayed) on at least one device; server-side interface status/counters not confirmed.\n\nPotential issues identified (with details)\n1) Branch firewall policy blocking ICMP (and possibly all traffic) between outside 10.0.0.0/30 and inside 10.0.1.0/24\n- Evidence:\n  - Both directions (10.1.0.2 \u2194 10.0.1.2) fail completely.\n  - Traceroute reaches branch-router but nothing beyond; hop 2 (expected branch-fw) returns no TTL-exceeded or any ICMP.\n  - No firewall ruleset visibility; default-deny or missing permit policies are common causes.\n- Impact:\n  - Outside-to-inside ICMP echo requests dropped; inside-to-outside echos/replies dropped; likely affects all protocols, not just ICMP.\n\n2) Routing on branch-fw and/or branch-router missing or incorrect for remote-worker or server subnets\n- Evidence:\n  - For return traffic from server to 10.1.0.2, branch-fw must have a route to 10.1.0.0/30 via 10.0.0.1 (branch-router). If absent, replies will be unroutable and may be silently dropped (especially with strict RPF).\n  - On branch-router, there must be a route to 10.0.1.0/24 via 10.0.0.2; if missing, router could drop or misroute toward default.\n  - Lack of any ICMP unreachable messages in traceroute suggests drop rather than explicit \u201cunreachable,\u201d consistent with firewall default-drop behavior, but misrouting remains a plausible contributor.\n- Impact:\n  - Asymmetric or blackholed paths resulting in 100% loss both ways.\n\n3) Interface/link state or addressing issue on branch-fw outside (10.0.0.2/30) or inside (10.0.1.1/24)\n- Evidence:\n  - Not confirmed; no counters or interface states available for these segments.\n  - If the firewall outside or inside interface were down/misaddressed, traffic would fail at/before hop 2.\n- Impact:\n  - Forwarding across the firewall would fail entirely.\n\n4) Host-based firewall on server (10.0.1.2) blocking ICMP\n- Evidence:\n  - Not verifiable; iptables/nft tooling missing on observed Alpine hosts. However, this alone would not explain failure in the server\u2192remote-worker direction unless egress ICMP is also blocked.\n- Impact:\n  - Could account for one direction; unlikely to fully explain the symmetric failure without matching egress/ingress blocks.\n\n5) Observability/diagnostic gaps due to command failures\n- Evidence:\n  - Multiple \u201ctimeout/error/command not found\u201d on iptables, nftables, ip -s link, traceroute invocations.\n- Impact:\n  - Hinders precise fault isolation; increases MTTR and risk of misdiagnosis.\n\nRecommended remediation and next steps\nA) Branch firewall (highest priority)\n- Validate interfaces and addressing:\n  - Confirm outside: 10.0.0.2/30, inside: 10.0.1.1/24 are UP and passing traffic; check ARP tables:\n    - ARP for 10.0.0.1 (branch-router) on outside.\n    - ARP for 10.0.1.2 (server) on inside.\n- Validate routing:\n  - Ensure route to 10.1.0.0/30 via 10.0.0.1 (branch-router).\n  - Ensure route to 10.0.1.0/24 is connected on inside.\n- Validate and adjust policy:\n  - Permit ICMP echo-request/echo-reply bidirectionally between:\n    - Source 10.1.0.2 (or 10.1.0.0/30) and destination 10.0.1.2 (or 10.0.1.0/24).\n  - If stateful, ensure the policy allows outside\u2192inside where required (default-deny is common for outside\u2192inside).\n  - Temporarily add an any/any permit with counters for troubleshooting to confirm the firewall is the choke point; then refine to least privilege.\n- Validate NAT:\n  - Ensure no NAT rule is inadvertently applied between these internal subnets; typically there should be no NAT for 10.1.0.0/30\u219410.0.1.0/24.\n- Observe counters/logs:\n  - Enable counters/logging on drop rules to confirm packets are hitting policy; review logs for drops of ICMP or these subnets.\n\nB) Branch router\n- Check connectivity to the firewall:\n  - Ping 10.0.0.2 from 10.0.0.1; show ARP for 10.0.0.2.\n- Verify routes:\n  - Static route to 10.0.1.0/24 via 10.0.0.2 (if not using a dynamic protocol).\n- Confirm no ACLs/ZBF are blocking ICMP toward 10.0.0.2/10.0.1.0/24.\n- Run traceroute (ICMP/UDP) toward 10.0.1.2 to see where it stops from the router\u2019s perspective.\n\nC) Server (10.0.1.2)\n- Verify interface state and addressing:\n  - eth0 should be UP with 10.0.1.2/24; default gateway should be 10.0.1.1.\n- Check host firewall:\n  - If using iptables/nft/ufw/firewalld, ensure ICMP is allowed from 10.1.0.2/30 and to 10.1.0.2/30.\n  - If tools are missing, install or use an equivalent method to verify kernel filter tables.\n- Basic tests:\n  - Ping 10.0.1.1 (firewall inside) and 10.1.0.2 (remote-worker). If ping to 10.0.1.1 fails, the issue is local to the inside segment or firewall.\n\nD) Restore observability (to avoid blind spots)\n- Ensure the troubleshooting environment has working tools/privileges:\n  - On Alpine hosts: install iproute2 (full), nftables, iptables as needed (e.g., apk add iproute2 nftables iptables).\n  - Ensure sudo privileges and non-BusyBox variants where full output is required.\n- Re-run:\n  - ip addr; ip -s link show; ip route; ip neigh; ping and traceroute (ICMP and UDP); nft list ruleset or iptables -L -nv on the firewall.\n\nQuick validation sequence after changes\n- From remote-worker:\n  - ping 10.1.0.1 (should succeed), then traceroute -I 10.0.1.2 (should pass hop 2), then ping 10.0.1.2.\n- From server:\n  - ping 10.0.1.1, ping 10.1.0.2, traceroute -I 10.1.0.2.\n- On firewall:\n  - Show policy counters increasing on permit rules between these subnets; ensure drop counters are not incrementing for this traffic.\n\nBottom line\n- The remote-worker side is healthy to the first hop; the failure localizes to the branch firewall or its adjacent links/policies. Start by enabling/validating routes and explicit permit rules for 10.1.0.0/30 \u2194 10.0.1.0/24 on the branch firewall, verifying interface state/ARP, and ensuring no unintended NAT. Address the tooling/visibility gaps so rule hits/drops and interface states can be confirmed."